```matlab
% function_name.m (one function per file, or local functions at end of script)
function result = factorial(n)
    if n <= 1
        result = 1;
    else
        result = n * factorial(n - 1);
    end
end

% Multiple outputs
function [mn, mx] = minmax(v)
    mn = min(v);
    mx = max(v);
end
[a, b] = minmax([3 1 4 1 5 9]);

% nargin / nargout — check number of arguments
function result = flexible(a, b, c)
    if nargin < 2, b = 1; end
    if nargin < 3, c = 0; end
    result = a * b + c;
end

% varargin — variable number of inputs
function result = sum_all(varargin)
    result = 0;
    for i = 1:nargin
        result = result + varargin{i};
    end
end
```

- Each function must be in a file with the same name (e.g., `factorial.m`), or defined as a local function at the bottom of a script
- MATLAB functions can return multiple outputs using brackets: `[a, b] = func(...)`
- `nargin` gives the number of arguments actually passed by the caller; use it to set defaults

```matlab
% Cell arrays — heterogeneous containers
c = {1, 'hello', [1 2 3], true}
c{1}       % 1
c{2}       % 'hello'
c{3}(2)    % 2
c{end}     % true

% Struct — named fields
s.name = 'Alice';
s.age  = 30;
s.scores = [85 90 92];

% Array of structs
s(2).name = 'Bob';
s(2).age  = 25;
[s.age]    % extract all ages: [30 25]
```

- Cell arrays use `{}` for indexing (not `()`); `()` selects a sub-cell-array, `{}` extracts the contents
- Structs are created by direct field assignment; no declaration needed
- `[s.field]` collects the value of `field` from every element of a struct array into a single array
- Cell arrays are useful when you need to store mixed types together (numbers, strings, arrays, etc.)
